  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A Noreaster will impact at least Downeast Maine\nthis weekend into Monday, bringing gusty winds, high surf, and \nperiods of rain.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nWe are still expecting a strong Noreaster impacting our area\nfrom Friday through the weekend and into Monday. However, we \nwill be on the far northern fringe of the Noreaster with the \ncenter staying south of Massachusetts through Sunday, and \nimpacts this far north through Sunday will be much less than \nareas further south. Here is what to expect for the various \ncomponents of the storm:\n\nPeriods of Rain...Rain chances will be far greater Downeast than\nnorthern areas for the storm, with rain possibly developing \nlate Friday night, but more likely on Saturday, and persisting \noff and on through Monday. There is still substantial \nuncertainty on the northern extent of the rain. Most likely \nDowneast up to Bangor will see periods of rain through the \nweekend, but totals could be anywhere from one quarter inch to \ntwo inches, as there is a ton of uncertainty. Even with the \nhigher totals, we would not expect flooding. Sunday night and \nMonday, a much weaker version of the Noreaster finally lifts \nnorth and tracks into the Gulf of Maine, at which point rain \nchances finally increase over the whole area. That said, not \nexpecting anything heavy.\n\nGusty Winds...Winds from the northeast will increase Friday\nnight into Saturday, remain gusty through Sunday, then decrease\na bit into Monday. Winds will be stronger the further south you\ngo, with gusts topping out around only 20 mph in Northern Maine\nthis weekend, but 25-35 mph Bangor and Downeast. We don't\nanticipate impacts from the wind other than maybe a very\nisolated outage Downeast.\n\nHigh Surf...Seas near the shore will be fairly rough with 5-9 ft\nseas, much of it as a longer period south/southeast swell. We\nare not expecting impactful coastal flooding, though waves\nbreaking on the rocks will be hazardous for any onlookers.\nConditions should be a bit more hazardous for coastal Hancock\nthan coastal Washington. Conditions slowly ease Sunday night \ninto Monday.",
